{"timestamp":"2026-11-24T06:50:30.487Z","ordinal":108347,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0f4fd85789ec756b016b0533b4b69c87d2bff7a6119c63d3f1","status":"completed","call_id":"call_cbc8ba30f19944958fafa385e614de20","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '1,125p' docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md; sed -n '125,210p' docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md\",workdir:\"/home/mike/nexus-wt/context-maturity-v130\",yield_time_ms:10000,max_output_tokens:6000}); text(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a20c49-1bcf-7563-9889-443e19f9956a","create_time":1795503028.715344}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":48710,"sampled_through_sequence":48708,"client_authored":false}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-24T06:50:30.503Z","ordinal":108348,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a20c49-1bcf-7563-9889-443e19f9956a","session_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","root_turn_id":"01a20c49-1bcf-7563-9889-443e19f9956a","response_id":"resp_0f4fd85789ec756b016b0533b2ac2c87d2b3cf2e31e688fd86","usage":{"input_tokens":117224,"cached_input_tokens":115840,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":165,"reasoning_output_tokens":59,"total_tokens":117389},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":4069734,"cached_input_tokens":3983488,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":24173,"reasoning_output_tokens":12522,"total_tokens":4093907},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":2325264184,"cached_input_tokens":2306966656,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":3822250,"reasoning_output_tokens":1837916,"total_tokens":2329086434}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-24T06:50:31.387Z","ordinal":108349,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a20c49-1bcf-7563-9889-443e19f9956a","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-a7a70828-f528-4ff9-8309-1b87cff6e0c9","process_id":"20471","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,125p' docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md; sed -n '125,210p' docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/context-maturity-v130","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,125p' docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md","name":"CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md","path":"docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '125,210p' docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md","name":"CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md","path":"docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"# Nexus context maturity and inventory\n\nStatus: implemented diagnostic, provenance, and request-observability layer on `release/v1.3.0`. Exact content-free request manifests are captured at the existing adapter-dispatch boundary; they do not replace request construction, token admission, or provider adapters.\n\n## Baseline\n\nRepository: Nexus Code. The initial operator checkout was clean `main` at `9156571f7d5fd8f8436c3105f0237628ab35e857`; the requested implementation target is `release/v1.3.0`, initially at `79ebb92c7254340effd5cd296181794d665648ae`. The isolated branch `codex/context-maturity-v130` was rebased as `release/v1.3.0` advanced, most recently onto `7ed3905930551be82b91f11a2aa4abfeec96a99f` (PR #748). That rebase completed without conflicts. The nested-Make classification fix, regression test, skill-scope freshness correction, and qualification updates are committed. Four untracked local trace files remain under `cli/.local/`; they are preserved and excluded from commits. The owner checkout remains untouched. Changes remain isolated and unpublished.\n\nThe reference is *The Context Engineering Maturity Model: A Framework for Building Production-Ready Agentic Systems* (Redis, 2025/2026 ebook), especially its four pillars and five descriptive stages (pp. 7–16). Nexus uses four dimensions requested for this assessment and adapts the stage language to a local coding client. Vendor architecture recommendations are not treated as implementation requirements.\n\n| Dimension | Baseline level | Evidence and observed behavior | Gaps and limitations | Next improvement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Navigability | 2 — Exploratory | `AutonomousAgent.requestMessages` carries conversation and pins the durable objective (`cli/src/agent.ts:2318-2338`); `getContextBudget` accounts for messages and tool schemas (`agent.ts:2475-2478`); repository search, skills, and `KnowledgeGraph` exist (`cli/src/knowledge.ts:37-45`, `cli/src/skills.ts:498-518`). | These sources do not have a shared inspectable inventory; the prompt combines several sources and history is replayed as messages. Retrieval reasons and cross-source relationships are not reported together. | Add a common inventory format and a read-only inspection command, while explicitly listing uncovered sources. |\n| Retrieval speed | 2 — Exploratory | Deterministic client token estimates, provider-advertised counts where available, admission and bounded compaction exist (`cli/src/tokens.ts:97-158`, `cli/src/agent.ts:3410-3507`). | No source-level retrieval latency budget or SLO is recorded. Admission logs do not measure time spent assembling each source; estimates are not provider measurements. | Record per-source and total inventory-assembly duration; keep it distinct from inference latency and token estimates. |\n| Freshness | 1 — Ad hoc | Context is gathered from the live session; candidate/verification evidence has identity-based invalidation elsewhere in the execution model. | Ordinary transcript/tool observations do not consistently carry a repository fingerprint or invalidation rule. Persisted knowledge has timestamps but no enforced applicability fingerprint. Unknown freshness can look current if rendered as prose. | Attach explicit freshness state and source references; report `unknown` where no applicable fingerprint exists. |\n| Compounding value | 2 — Exploratory | Sessions, compaction archives and durable task state persist across process restarts (`cli/src/session.ts:102-184`); project knowledge persists in `.nexus/knowledge.json` (`cli/src/knowledge.ts:37-45`). | Session history, task summaries, and repository knowledge have different scopes but no unified provenance/retention view. Knowledge auto-extraction is opt-in and trust-gated, but a stored entry is not evidence of correctness. | Preserve source-only inventory metadata in session records and exports; do not elevate model or tool claims to verified facts. |\n\n## Implemented assessment\n\nThe current `/context maturity` output is computed from one content-free on-demand inventory and can change with observed session evidence. On the deterministic command fixture used here, it reports navigability 2 — Exploratory, retrieval speed 2 — Exploratory, freshness 1 — Ad hoc, and compounding value 1 — Ad hoc (no durable memory was present in that fixture). Ratings remain independent and do not claim a managed maturity level.\n\n| Dimension | Current level | Evidence now available | Remaining gap / next improvement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Navigability | 2 — Exploratory | `/context inspect [--json]` reports source identity, scope, trust, freshness, retrieval reason/method, and coverage gaps. Bounded inference manifests reference the exact message positions and tool schemas handed to the adapter; current in-memory system-prompt sections link to `request:message:0`; session records and exports preserve the request references. | Message references identify position and role, not a stable semantic relationship graph. Prompt-section links are invalidated after prompt bytes change and are unavailable after resume until the prompt is rebuilt. The manifest proves the client-side handoff attempt, not provider receipt or processing. |\n| Retrieval speed | 2 — Exploratory | Inspection reports per-source and total local collection timings, a configurable total deadline (default 2 seconds), timeout/cancellation outcomes, item omissions, and client-estimated tokens. Request manifests report configured/advertised limits; the last request count/source remains separate when available. Manifest construction now computes the aggregate request estimate while collecting per-message details, avoiding a second full tokenization pass. A synthetic alternating microbenchmark (64 messages, 12 tools, 4 iterations per sample, 8 samples) measured median 54.37 ms for the previous two-pass path and 26.99 ms for the current path (50.4% lower local manifest-build time). | The microbenchmark does not establish end-to-end TUI throughput or inference latency. Diagnostic collection still does not measure live request preparation distributions; synchronous readers cannot be preempted; no cache telemetry or measured retrieval SLO exists. |\n| Freshness | 1 — Ad hoc | Items carry explicit `current` / `stale` / `unknown` plus invalidation rules. Session and archive reconstruction marks history unknown. `/context inspect` records a bounded composite candidate fingerprint and compares it with the previous saved inventory; changed workspaces produce invalidated repository-item references and an explicit partial-coverage reason. Durable knowledge entries now explicitly report unknown freshness in inspection and prompt text because their opaque source references have no stored fingerprint or revalidation. | There is no per-source freshness identity for ignored environment inputs, tool policy, or individual memory provenance, and no measured change-to-invalidation objective. Memory freshness remains unknown; the system does not infer staleness or correctness from an opaque reference. |\n| Compounding value | 1 — Ad hoc in the no-memory fixture and when only a project-knowledge catalog entry is present; 2 — Exploratory only when a durable note or compaction link is observed in carried session context | Session state, compaction links, and bounded request manifests persist source references without copying context text. Operator-only promotion preserves provenance, unverified validation state, repository scope and retention; legacy entries migrate as unverified; expiry is enforced; model writes and automatic extraction are disabled. | Held-out evidence that persistent memory improves engineering outcomes is not measured; the existing durable knowledge store has repository scope only. |\n\nRatings are per dimension. There is no aggregate maturity grade. A feature's presence alone does not satisfy a stage: the report requires evidence for shared behavior, freshness, and measured service levels.\n\n## Design decision record\n\n### Decision\n\nAdd a small, local context inventory and deterministic maturity assessment that reuse `AutonomousAgent` getters, `DurableTaskState`/`SessionManager`, existing token estimates, `KnowledgeGraph`, and the common slash router. The inventory stores metadata and token estimates, not prompt plaintext or content hashes. Existing provider adapters remain the only inference transport boundary; this feature does not call providers.\n\n### Data model and interfaces\n\n- `ContextInventoryItem`: stable source-identity id (source type + location + scope; no content hash is persisted), source kind/location, estimated tokens, observed time, freshness (`current`, `stale`, `unknown`), trust state, retention, inclusion reason, retrieval method, and optional source references. Message and tool-call identifiers are retained only as opaque hashes.\n- `ContextInventorySnapshot`: schema version, capture time, session/task/workspace identity, optional bounded composite workspace fingerprint and comparison outcome, item list, per-source timing/status/counts, estimate provenance, omissions, completeness, and reason for incomplete or unknown coverage. Timeout/cancellation is an additive `outcome` value while the existing `status` remains `failed`, so schema-1 readers continue to accept the inventory.\n- `InferenceContextManifest`: bounded latest-24 session records created immediately before `submitGuardingLength` calls the adapter. It stores message position/role references, effective tool-schema positions/counts, client token estimate, context/output budgets and their sources, and dispatch outcome. Provider token-count observations remain in the existing separate token-accounting record because the code does not bind every such measurement to the exact same serialized arrays. Prompt and schema text, raw request IDs, model/provider identity, and content hashes are not persisted.\n- The persisted snapshot is optional. Older session records remain valid; inventory reconstruction labels transcript freshness unknown and marks session-save coverage partial because the exact provider request is unavailable.\n- `/context inspect [--json] [--record] [--budget-ms <1..30000>] [--max-items <1..500>]` measures current client-visible sources on demand and optionally stores a content-free session snapshot; `/context maturity [--json]` accepts the same budget/item limits and emits a deterministic four-dimension assessment with evidence, gaps, limitations, and next improvements. The default total source-collection budget is 2,000 ms. The same budget applies sequentially across the whole collection pass, so source order is deterministic and remaining sources are marked timed out after exhaustion.\n- `AutonomousAgent.getSystemPromptContextSources()` exposes temporary prompt slices derived from the active system message using captured byte offsets. It includes identifiable sections (few-shots, TDD policy, selected role, project rules, skill catalog, and durable project knowledge) plus residual runtime-policy sections, all linked to `request:message:0`. Only source metadata is persisted. A SHA-256 digest is used in memory only to refuse stale offsets; prompt mutation or restore invalidates the slice mapping, with the opaque system message retained as a conservative fallback.\n\n### Compatibility, security, privacy, and retention\n\n- Existing message and session schemas remain readable. New metadata is additive and source-only; full message and tool content remains in the existing session/archive path.\n- Hashes and locators are diagnostic provenance, not proof that a tool observation is true. Model output is labeled model-generated; tool output is labeled unverified unless an existing verifier receipt binds it.\n- No secret-bearing source text or per-file content hashes are copied into the inventory or maturity report. A composite candidate fingerprint supports equality comparison within the saved session; it does not reveal a file path or claim correctness. Paths are workspace-relative where possible. Inventory snapshots follow the containing session's existing retention/deletion behavior; no cross-user cache is introduced.\n- Workspace changes do not silently make an old observation current. If Nexus has no compatible source fingerprint, freshness remains `unknown`.\n- Inventory collection is local and read-only. Failed or unsupported sources are reported with explicit partial status.\n- Durable project knowledge is promoted only through explicit operator commands. Model-invoked `manage_knowledge` rejects `set` even through the internal handler, and the model-visible schema omits that action. Legacy conversation auto-extraction is a no-op, including when its old environment flag is present.\n- Knowledge entries retain repository scope, provenance, validation status, timestamps, and retention policy. Legacy entries migrate additively as `legacy_unverified`; expiring entries are removed from retrieval and pruned on load. Prompt injection labels all knowledge as unverified memory, never evidence. Secret-bearing values are rejected before entering the prompt-visible map.\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n\n1. The same session state yields the same item identities, estimates, assessment levels, and evidence ordering (capture timestamps and duration metrics are the only variable fields).\n2. Inventory content contains no source plaintext and distinguishes operator, model, tool, and persisted-knowledge trust states. A non-unknown freshness claim requires an explicit invalidation rule; persistence or resume without revalidation demotes `current` to `unknown`. A bounded current candidate fingerprint is compared with the previous capture; changed state reports prior repository-scoped references as invalidated, while missing fingerprints remain unknown.\n3. Compaction and resume retain predecessor source references or mark reconstructed provenance and freshness as unknown; exports carry the inventory and archive references.\n4. Source errors, cancellation, timeout, and item caps are visible as incomplete with counts; no partial inventory is called complete. The configurable deadline applies to the whole local source-collection pass. Promise-based readers are raced against the remaining budget and late results are discarded; synchronous readers cannot be preempted, so an over-budget result is discarded and labeled with `outcome: timed_out` after return. Assembly timing includes session read, source collection, and inventory construction.\n5. Active prompt source links resolve against the exact current message bytes, and inventory/export persist no prompt text. Replaced/restored prompts cannot reuse captured offsets; absence of a valid map is surfaced as a coverage gap and the system message is inventoried opaquely.\n6. Context inspection and maturity assessment are available through the shared REPL/TUI slash router and JSON output is parseable.\n7. A schema-less/legacy session remains loadable and receives conservative reconstructed inventory metadata; malformed request manifests are discarded.\n8. No provider changes, provider-specific headers, or new service dependencies are added.\n9. Model tool calls and legacy automatic extraction cannot promote durable memory; operator-created knowledge carries provenance and explicit unverified validation status, and expired entries are not retrieved.\n10. A request manifest is durably written before adapter dispatch when a configured sink exists; a configured durable-write failure prevents that request from being sent. It receives `response_received` only after the adapter returns and is reconciled to `unknown_effect` after failure or restart before response persistence.\n\n### Implemented boundary and deferred work\n\n- After the owner authorized takeover of stale claims, request capture was integrated at `AutonomousAgent.submitGuardingLength`; the same content-free manifest is saved through existing task/session persistence, exposed to `/context inspect`, and included in session exports. Recovered `dispatching` records become `unknown_effect`; no claim is made that this client-side record proves provider receipt.\n- Automatic memory learning, retrieval ranking changes, and context eviction remain deferred. Durable promotion is operator-only; accumulated memory has not yet been shown to improve task quality on held-out work.\n- No Redis, MCP server, vector database, new provider extension, or autonomous memory learner is justified by measured requirements.\n\n## Reference coding\n\nXERJ retrieval on `nexus-cli` located the existing context admission/token-accounting path in `cli/src/agent.ts` (`requestMessages`, `admitContext`), the `manage_knowledge` capability (`cli/src/core/capability_definitions/knowledge.ts:23`), the local project knowledge graph (`cli/src/knowledge.ts:37-45`), and `computeCandidateIdentitySync` (`cli/src/core/candidate.ts:425`, class method at line 979). The XERJ candidate lookup was lexical; direct branch inspection confirmed that the candidate identity includes the base commit and bounded changed workspace files. OpenCode transport code distinguishes request/read timeout failures and leaves the total-request budget opt-in (`packages/ai/src/route/transport/http.ts:108`); Nexus adopts an explicit caller-selected inspection budget and truthful timeout outcomes, without copying peer code. Nexus keeps the existing `KnowledgeGraph`, removes model-write affordances at both schema and handler, reuses candidate identity for freshness comparison, and rejects a second planner, provider-specific context protocol, external retrieval service, or copied peer implementation. XERJ indexes committed `origin/main`; the active worktree was checked directly.\n\n## Verification evidence\n\nImplementation worktree: `codex/context-maturity-v130`. The feature branch began from `origin/release/v1.3.0` at `a57c6c24` and was rebased onto the current release base `10942d410875f09e469cf7b39979cfa9c336974b`; changes remain local, with no push or PR publication.\n\n| Check | Result | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| `npm run build` (from `cli/`) | Passed after request-manifest integration | TypeScript type-check plus CLI data/build-info generation steps. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/context_inventory.test.ts` | 16 passed, 0 failed on the current prompt-provenance and context-budget implementation | Content-free exact-array manifest capture, prompt-section byte slices and digest invalidation, dispatch-before-persistence refusal, failure/restart `unknown_effect`, privacy validation, context assessment, session/export round-trip, async timeout/cancellation, sync overrun discard, error redaction, invalid budget, and deterministic item cap. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/knowledge_memory_controls.test.ts` | 3 passed, 0 failed | Operator-only promotion, provenance/validation/retention metadata, additive legacy migration, expiry enforcement, internal model-handler denial. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/persistence_protection.test.ts` | 27 passed, 0 failed | Credential rejection before prompt-visible or durable memory; session, workspace, checkpoint, export and persistence protections. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/slash_router.test.ts` | 39 passed, 0 failed | Shared slash catalogue, help, dispatch, REPL/TUI parity, session/workspace/extension routing. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/session_durable_fields.test.ts` | 4 passed, 0 failed | Session round-trip and REPL/sidecar/ApplicationCore durable-state integration. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/session_export_escaping.test.ts` | 5 passed, 0 failed | Existing export escaping regressions. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/harness.test.ts` | 90 passed, 0 failed | Agent execution, context budgeting/compaction, provider recovery fixtures, workspace behavior, cancellation, and worktree isolation. This is one test file, not the full repository suite. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/provider_wire_matrix.test.ts` | 90 passed, 0 failed | Local mock wire fixtures across OpenAI-compatible, Anthropic-compatible, and other adapters; no live inference or provider modification. |\n| `npm run build` (rebased implementation HEAD `10cccb5d`) | Passed | TypeScript build after rebasing onto `release/v1.3.0` at `cd755b3e`. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/context_inventory.test.ts` (rebased implementation HEAD `10cccb5d`) | 16 passed, 0 failed | Rechecked request provenance, prompt inventory, timeout/budget outcomes, memory/session boundaries, and command integration after the release rebase. |\n| `node scripts/run_tests.mjs test/eval_dataset.test.ts` (rebased branch; evaluator helper restored to committed mode 0644) | 3 passed, 0 failed | The test initially failed because the shared checkout materialized `eval/scan-compiler-exposure.mjs` as group-writable (0664), which the evaluator correctly rejects. Correcting only the local file mode to the committed non-writable mode made the provider-stimulus and hidden-validator checks pass; no source or assertion was changed. |\n| `NEXUS_TEST_FILE_TIMEOUT_SECS=1200 node scripts/run_tests.mjs test/compaction_continuity.test.ts` (qualification snapshot `2502ae1b`) | 34 passed, 0 failed, 540.6 seconds | The first full run used the runner's 300-second default and timed out this file while its first test was still progressing. Its explicit test timeout is 900 seconds. Raising only the watchdog allowed the whole file to complete without changing test behavior. |\n| `make test` (detached snapshot `48bb93f2`, before runtime build outputs were placed in expected worktree paths) | Failed overall: Rust core 53 passed, TUI 406 passed, CLI 1,796 completed with 117 failures and 2 timeouts; scope fixtures and security gates passed. | The run reported `harness-core is not built`, `cli/dist is not built`, and repeated core RPC handshake failures. This establishes an invalid/incomplete test setup for affected CLI cases, but does not attribute every CLI failure to that setup. |\n| `make test` (corrected detached snapshot `2502ae1b`, based on `a5faa878`, with `make build` outputs in the expected paths) | Failed overall, exit 2. Core 53/53, TUI 406/406, scope fixtures, and security lifecycle gates passed. The CLI shard reported 3 failures among 2,493 completed segments and timed out `compaction_continuity.test.ts`. | The run had the compiled CLI and Rust core at expected paths. Failure output includes `eval_dataset.test.ts` (“provider request contains the selected stimulus”); the shard also reported an `ERR_INVALID_ARG_TYPE` and further assertion failure(s), which were not fully recovered from the sharded output. This is not a clean full-suite qualification; the failures need triage. |\n| `node --import tsx --test test/turn_semantics.test.ts` (snapshot `48bb93f2`, after `make build` and expected worktree binary placement) | 37 passed, 0 failed | Repeated the full turn-semantics file with the Rust core and CLI build outputs available at the locations used by the CLI test harness. |\n| `node --import tsx --test --test-name-pattern='progressing task runs past 30 steps' test/step_loop.test.ts` (snapshot `48bb93f2`, after runtime build outputs were placed) | 1 passed, 0 failed | Focused rerun of a step-loop case that had failed in the incomplete full-suite setup. |\n| Focused re-audit (`npm run build`; `node --import tsx --test test/context_inventory.test.ts`) | Build passed; 11 passed, 0 failed | Rechecked command inspection against persisted inference manifests and removed the obsolete claim that per-inference capture was unavailable. |\n| Workspace freshness invalidation (`npm run build`; `node --import tsx --test test/context_inventory.test.ts`) | Build passed; 14 passed, 0 failed | Bounded agent candidate identity changes when tracked content changes; changed-state comparison, invalidated repository references, unavailable-state fallback, legacy snapshots, and session persistence are covered. |\n| `git diff --check` | Passed after documentation and implementation changes | Whitespace/error check on the implementation worktree. |\n\nAll focused TypeScript test commands used Node 24.11.1, one file at a time, with isolated `HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`. `make build` passed in the detached snapshot with an isolated Cargo target directory; its runtime outputs were copied into that snapshot's ignored `core/target/release` directory because the test harness resolves binaries there. The corrected full run therefore supersedes the earlier incomplete-run caveat, but failed in the CLI shard as detailed above. No inference provider was modified or contacted by this feature.\n\nAfter rebasing onto PR #739, focused checks passed on the rebased source: context inventory 14/14, provider wire matrix 90/90, agent harness 90/90, slash router 39/39, knowledge controls 3/3, session durable fields 4/4, and persistence protection 27/27. The CLI build passed. On snapshot `48bb93f2` (based on `cebb31ac`, before PR #742), the full turn-semantics suite passed 37/37 and the focused long-step case passed 1/1 after the runtime binaries were placed where the CLI resolves them. After rebasing onto PR #742 at `a57c6c24`, exact-current-source checks passed on `b588d970`: CLI build, context inventory 14/14, knowledge controls 3/3, and provider wire matrix 90/90. At `03b62aaf`, `make build` passed, then `make test` was run with isolated CI-like workspace/runtime fixtures. Rust core passed 53/53, and scope fixtures/security gates passed; the aggregate command failed. Its aggregate test count was not available from the sharded runner output. The isolated `eval_dataset.test.ts` and standalone server-profile eval both fail closed before inference with `compiler exposure could not be checked; refusing qualification`; the gateway saw only the model-list probe, not a chat request. The version-probe test initially rejected the temporary `/tmp` runtime root; a retry used `/opt/nexus-eval-ci-runtime` and a private 128 MiB tmpfs with the required owner-only mount mode, but the host's bubblewrap could not access the workspace through its `/tmp` parent path. No protected fixture or guard was changed to bypass these checks. The D-04 behavior test exposed an obsolete assertion that expected model-originated `manage_knowledge` to persist durable memory. The test now asserts that this call is rejected and creates no knowledge file; `turn_verdict_evidence.test.ts` passes 26/26. After rebasing onto the latest `origin/release/v1.3.0` at `10942d41`, the focused checks before budget work passed: context inventory 14/14, knowledge controls 3/3, provider wire matrix 90/90, and turn verdict 26/26. The bounded-budget and prompt-provenance implementation passed `npm run build`, `context_inventory.test.ts` 16/16, knowledge controls 3/3, provider wire matrix 90/90, slash router 39/39, persistence protection 27/27, and skill injection 15/15 on exact snapshot `2502ae1b`. The corrected `make test` on snapshot `2502ae1b` passed core 53/53, TUI 406/406, scope fixtures and security gates, but failed overall in the CLI shard: 2,493 completed segments, 3 failures, and a timeout in `compaction_continuity.test.ts`. Focused reruns show the dataset failure came from the local shared-checkout mode of the trusted evaluator helper, and the compaction file completes when the watchdog allows its declared 900-second test timeout. Two of the three CLI failures remain unclassified because the shard output was truncated. The full suite has not been rerun after correcting those local conditions or after rebasing onto `cd755b3e`; repository-wide qualification remains pending.\n\n### Remaining acceptance gaps\n\n- The request manifest records client-side arrays handed to adapters, not provider receipt, preprocessing, or internal provider-side prompt state. Message references are positional; active system-prompt links cover identifiable client sections, while semantic source links for other message content are not yet modeled.\n- The total source-collection deadline is enforceable for asynchronous readers; JavaScript cannot interrupt a synchronous read, so those are detected and discarded after they return. Source-level percentiles, cache telemetry, and a retrieval SLO are not yet measured.\n- Freshness compares bounded repository candidate fingerprints for saved session inventories, but does not cover ignored environment inputs, tool policy, provider-side context, or the underlying sources of individual memory claims. Memory freshness remains explicitly unknown; inventories now compare a bounded metadata version (record update timestamp plus validation status) and flag a prior captured record when either changes. That detects record mutation, not whether the remembered claim remains true. Invalidation latency has not been measured. The candidate scan is bounded by 4,096 manifest entries and 16 MiB of changed content, with Git's configured timeout; failures produce unknown freshness and partial coverage. No retrieval-latency SLO or cache instrumentation exists.\n- Workspace skill entries use repository scope so a candidate change lists them as invalidated; skill file content itself remains explicitly unknown until it is revalidated. Profile-level skill freshness, tool-policy freshness, and memory provenance freshness are also unknown.\n- Operator-only memory promotion now carries metadata and expiry enforcement, but no held-out task-quality evaluation establishes that durable memories improve results. Existing schema-1 records are migrated additively when a writable load occurs; read-only loads label them in memory without rewriting their files.\n- No independent review has been completed. The earlier full-suite attempt on snapshot `2502ae1b` was interrupted after more than two hours while CLI integration shards continued; several failures had appeared, and no aggregate was produced. Aggregate qualification remains pending.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-23, release rebase and retry classification\n\nThe completed full `make test` run on snapshot `a9ea0f19` (based on release head `a3f5971c`) reproduced two CLI failures in DV-1/DV-2 classification. Both were caused by GNU Make's nested `make[1]: ... Error 127` output being missed by the environment-failure recognizer; the outer `make ... Error 2` was then incorrectly treated as a candidate failure. The fix now recognizes both plain and numbered `make` prefixes. A regression test asserts that the nested missing-command output is `ENVIRONMENT`, while a real test-runner failure appended to the same output remains `CANDIDATE_FAILED`.\n\nAfter rebasing onto `c02bb243` (release head observed at 2026-11-23 15:47 UTC), `npm --prefix cli run build` passed. Focused tests passed: `dv2_convergence.test.ts` 27/27, `dv1_structured_attempts.test.ts` 20/20 including the new regression, `context_inventory.test.ts` 17/17, `audit_export.test.ts` 17/17, `knowledge_memory_controls.test.ts` 3/3, `provider_wire_matrix.test.ts` 90/90, and `slash_router.test.ts` 39/39. These use local fixtures and no live inference. They do not replace a full suite.\n\nA fixed-head full-suite run on the rebased source has not started. The `SUITE` claim is held by an active AC-2 worktree and its mock gateway process was present during this update, so the suite was not run concurrently. A test setup attempt was interrupted before test execution when the claim conflict was discovered; its unique mount/runtime fixture paths were verified absent afterward. The fixed-source full suite, independent review, and any new exact-head checks remain outstanding. No provider changes were made.\nA fixed-head full-suite run on the rebased source has not started. The `SUITE` claim is held by an active AC-2 worktree and its mock gateway process was present during this update, so the suite was not run concurrently. A test setup attempt was interrupted before test execution when the claim conflict was discovered; its unique mount/runtime fixture paths were verified absent afterward. The fixed-source full suite, independent review, and any new exact-head checks remain outstanding. No provider changes were made.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-23, skill freshness scope\n\nThe context inventory previously labeled every skill catalog item `profile` scope, so repository candidate changes could not list workspace skills among the invalidated repository items. Workspace skill items now use `repository` scope, while global skills remain `profile`; both report freshness as `unknown` because the inventory does not re-read or persist a skill content digest. A regression assertion covers those scope labels and prior workspace-skill invalidation. `npm --prefix cli run typecheck` passed in a clean detached snapshot at `f7544181` using isolated `HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`; the targeted test is still unrun because the other worktree holds the shared `SUITE` claim. XERJ search found the existing skill content digest and qualification invalidation path (`cli/src/core/skill_activation.ts:67`, `cli/src/core/skills_qualification.ts:126`) and the catalog refresh/read implementation (`cli/src/skills.ts:219`, `cli/src/skills.ts:421`). We retain `unknown` rather than persist content digests in diagnostics, consistent with the context inventory's privacy boundary.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-23, release rebase\n\nAfter `release/v1.3.0` advanced to `7ed39059`, the implementation branch rebased cleanly. This release update adds workflow/cache CI changes and a workflow parity test; the source feature changes are unchanged. On detached exact-source snapshot `049a6077`, `npm --prefix cli run typecheck` and `make build` passed with isolated `HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, and `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`; Rust release and CLI builds completed. At 2026-11-23 16:11 UTC, the AC-2 claim owner PID and all processes referencing its worktree were absent; its mock gateway had also exited. The claim was still less than 24 hours old, so it was not broken. The `SUITE` claim was taken after it disappeared. Another process began a sequence of isolated CLI tests before our test reservation; the new skill-scope regression and full `make test` are waiting until that process finishes, to keep tests serial. No test result is inferred from the build.\n\n## Validation update — 2026-11-23\n\nOn exact source head `976ba97d` (base `db9f6f98`, `release/v1.3.0`): `make build` passed using isolated `HOME`/`XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and the worktree-local Cargo target. Under Node 24.11.1, `context_inventory.test.ts` passed 16/16, `knowledge_memory_controls.test.ts` passed 3/3, and `provider_wire_matrix.test.ts` passed 90/90. These tests cover deterministic assessment and insufficient evidence, request/source provenance through save/resume/compaction/export, bounded and failed retrieval, freshness invalidation, scoped durable memory and model-write denial, and the provider adapter request matrix. They do not establish provider receipt of manifests or held-out memory value.\n\nThe dedicated `tui_maturity_e2e.test.ts` was run on that same head and failed after 190.4 seconds: the pseudo-terminal driver did not observe a terminal result for the scripted 35-step turn within its 180-second wait; the TUI had reached step 18. After the driver deadline, fixture cleanup removed the workspace while the sidecar was still unwinding, which caused follow-on persistence and workspace-state errors in the terminal event. Those cleanup errors are consequences of the timeout and are not evidence that persistence failed during an uninterrupted run. A diagnostic-only comparison on the release baseline (`db9f6f98`) reached 22 tool cycles within the same 180-second driver wait; request spacing averaged 8.38 seconds on baseline versus 9.85 seconds on the candidate run, and mock provider response times were below 2 seconds in both traces. This is a possible client-side throughput regression signal, not a controlled attribution: the runs were sequential on a loaded shared host, and no repeat or randomized paired run was completed. The temporary trace instrumentation was removed and no product code was changed from this comparison. The actual cause of the slower candidate step rate is not established. Host load and unrelated agent activity are contemporaneous confounders, not proof of causation. TUI end-to-end qualification therefore remains failed/unverified; the timeout must not be counted as a pass, and the possible throughput regression needs a controlled follow-up.\n\nThe full `make test` run described above was on pre-rebase snapshot `2502ae1b`, not `976ba97d`. It was manually interrupted after more than two hours while progressing through remaining CLI tests, after multiple CLI failures appeared; no final shard summary or overall exit result was collected. This is an incomplete historical run, not a pass. The corrected, completed full-suite result on snapshot `0b9feaae` is recorded in the latest validation update below. No inference-provider changes or provider-specific behavior were introduced.\n\nOn exact source head `dafe4fa2` (base `db9f6f98`, `release/v1.3.0`), durable project knowledge context explicitly carries `freshness: unknown` and a reason in `/context inspect`; prompt rendering labels each retained entry `freshness: unknown` because its source references are not revalidated. Request manifest construction now derives the aggregate estimate from the same per-message and tool counts it records; a deterministic assertion matches `calculateContextBudget`, including tool calls and capped references. `npm run build` passed; `context_inventory.test.ts` passed 17/17; the provider wire matrix passed 90/90 on this head. `knowledge_memory_controls.test.ts` passed 3/3 and `slash_router.test.ts` 39/39 after the preceding memory-freshness change. A synthetic microbenchmark measured 50.4% lower local manifest-build time on its stated fixture; it is not an end-to-end throughput result. No source digest or memory invalidation mechanism was added.\n\nA TUI E2E rerun during this work failed before any inference activity: after five seconds the PTY driver expected `thinking · 0:0` but the screen still showed `waiting for the agent`. Subsequent persistence notices occurred during driver/fixture teardown; a preserved fixture's existing committed message log validated after teardown, so those notices do not establish an uninterrupted-run persistence defect. This run does not measure the optimized manifest path's effect on TUI throughput. TUI qualification and the possible end-to-end throughput regression remain open.\n\nOn current source/documentation head `5a5eb68a`, `harness.test.ts` passed 90/90 in 80.7 seconds with isolated `HOME`/`XDG_CONFIG_HOME`. It exercises the agent loop, context admission and compaction, worktree behavior, and simulated provider responses. This focused harness result does not replace the incomplete repository-wide test run or the failed PTY E2E check.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-23, TUI rerun\n\nOn the current candidate (`33e744c9`, based on `release/v1.3.0` at `db9f6f98`), `tui_maturity_e2e.test.ts` passed 1/1 in 70.1 seconds, including the scripted multi-step turn, context inspection, resume, and export assertions. The same E2E test then passed 1/1 on the release baseline (`db9f6f98`) in 47.0 seconds. This was one sequential pair on the same host, not a randomized or repeated benchmark; the candidate took about 23 seconds longer in this sample. Treat this as a throughput regression signal requiring controlled repeated runs, not a causal attribution or a qualification failure. It supersedes the earlier statement above that the latest PTY run was still failed/unverified: functional TUI E2E now passes, while comparative performance and full-suite qualification remain open. Earlier timeout runs remain recorded as historical failures.\n\nThe temporary failure-only PTY diagnostic was reverted after the candidate and baseline reruns. No inference provider or provider-specific behavior was changed. At that point the full repository suite had not been rerun on the current release base; see the later full-suite update. Independent review remains outstanding.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-23, full suite and export correction\n\n`make test-ci` initially stopped before running tests because the shared `/mnt/nexus-eval-ci` fixture already existed and was non-empty. It was left untouched. The same repository fixture script was then run against unique temporary mount/runtime paths, preserving its 128 MiB tmpfs and permissions. On snapshot `62350f89` (before the export correction below), `make build` passed; `cargo test` exited successfully; the CLI shard reported 2,930 tests (2,918 passed, 8 failed, 4 skipped) and timed out in `compaction_continuity.test.ts`; terminal-scope reported 3,140 tests (3,109 passed, 18 failed, 13 skipped); security gates passed. The overall `make test` exited 1. Several failures were host qualification/toolchain/resource checks; the audit-export determinism failure was attributable to this change and was reproduced in `audit_export.test.ts`.\n\n\nOn corrected snapshot `0b9feaae` (branch `codex/context-maturity-v130`, based on `release/v1.3.0` at `db9f6f98`), `make build` passed and the full `make test` completed with exit 2 using unique CI fixture roots; the existing shared `/mnt/nexus-eval-ci` was not modified. Core Rust tests passed 53/53; TUI unit tests passed 406/406; lifecycle security gates passed. The CLI shard reported 2,930 completed tests, 7 failures, and a timeout in `compaction_continuity.test.ts`. The terminal-scope shard reported 3,140 tests: 3,112 passed, 15 failed, and 13 skipped. Failures observed include host compiler-exposure checks refusing qualification, eval fixture/runtime assumptions, and convergence/attempt-classification assertions; two T3 end-to-end checks also failed earlier in the same run. These are preserved as failures and have not been individually reproduced or proven environmental. The export-specific regression files pass on this source (`audit_export.test.ts` 17/17 and `context_inventory.test.ts` 17/17), and the TypeScript build passes, but repository-wide qualification is not green. Independent review remains outstanding. No inference provider was modified.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-23, rebased v1.3.0 exact-head completion\n\nThis update supersedes earlier statements above that full-suite qualification on the current release branch was outstanding. The implementation branch was rebased without conflicts onto `origin/release/v1.3.0` at `f4333a359b142e7440a75932506a36d7dbabb924`. Exact feature head: `eda532061b8f78c67ddc3ffe4e3c3621312fd4eb`. `make build` passed on the source worktree; focused context, memory, provider-wire, routing and session/export tests passed 158/158 on this head.\n\nThe full `make test` was run to completion on a detached snapshot at that exact head, with isolated `HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, worktree-local Cargo outputs, and unique CI fixture/mount paths. It exited 0. Rust core passed 53/53; TUI passed 406/406. CLI shard one passed 3,165/3,169 with 4 skipped; CLI shard two passed 2,956/2,969 with 13 skipped. Terminal scope fixtures passed, and all lifecycle security gates passed, including dependency audits, secret scanning, diff hygiene, and documentation scanning. The early `ℹ fail` lines in nested fixture outputs are expected assertions from tests that verify rejection/failure behavior; both aggregate CLI shard summaries report zero failures. The existing shared CI fixture paths and owner checkout were not modified. No live inference was used and no inference provider was modified.\n\nThe detached validation snapshot used a dependency tree copied with symlinks preserved and the evaluator helper's local materialized file mode restored to the committed 0644 mode; neither correction changed tracked source. The run establishes local full-suite qualification for that exact source snapshot, not hosted CI, release qualification, installed acceptance, provider receipt of request manifests, or independent review. Claude owns review and merge from this point; no PR, push, merge, or tag was created here.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-23, durable-memory version invalidation\n\nOn rebased source head `40c2194a`, context inventory items carry a content-free source version made from the normalized record update timestamp and validation state. A new capture compares those markers with the previous saved inventory and reports when the prior memory record changed; the current memory claim remains `unknown` because its underlying evidence is not revalidated. Source references remain in their existing bounded field, and the marker survives session save/resume and JSON/Markdown export. Unsafe version metadata is omitted or normalized before it reaches diagnostics. `context_inventory.test.ts` passed 17/17 and `npm run build` passed with isolated `HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` on this exact head. The full `make test` result above predates this follow-up; it has not been rerun on `40c2194a`. The four maturity ratings do not change. No provider behavior changed.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-23, latest release rebase and full-suite result\n\nThe feature branch was rebased onto `origin/release/v1.3.0` at `56e644cf5f0b6d3c85b82dc3bfec958535ae2493`. On the then-current pre-rebase-follow-up snapshot `cabe335e83b2b4b870856e6edd949ae7d52f1489`, `make build` passed and `make test` completed with exit 2. Core Rust tests passed 53/53; TUI tests passed 406/406; one CLI shard reported 2,959 tests (2,945 passed, 1 failed, 13 skipped), and the other reported 3,179 tests (3,175 passed, 4 skipped). The failing assertion was `test/cloud_providers.test.ts`: operator cancellation during a streaming response exceeded its 2-second bound and failed after the test runner's 300-second timeout. Scope fixtures and lifecycle/security gates passed. This is a recorded failure, not a green full-suite result.\n\nThe cancellation test was then run five times sequentially with isolated `HOME` and ephemeral mock-provider ports; all five passed in 137–147 ms. Source inspection confirms the caller signal is wired into `StreamWatchdog`, which aborts the fetch signal, and `readSse` cancels its reader on error. These repeats do not explain the full-shard timeout, so the intermittent failure remains unresolved and must not be classified as environmental or fixed. On the subsequently rebased current source, focused tests passed: `context_inventory.test.ts` 17/17, `knowledge_memory_controls.test.ts` 3/3, `provider_wire_matrix.test.ts` (all cases passed), session persistence/export/fork test files (all passed), and `audit_export.test.ts` 18/18. `make build` also passed with isolated `HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, and a worktree-local `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`.\n\nThis rebase includes release-branch commits not present in the full-suite snapshot; full-suite status at the exact current head is therefore unverified. The four maturity ratings and their evidence remain unchanged. No inference provider was modified. Hosted checks, installed acceptance, a repeated comparative performance evaluation, and independent review remain outstanding; Claude owns PR review and merge. No push, PR, merge, or tag was performed.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-24, release tip refresh and focused requalification\n\nAfter the full-suite run, `origin/release/v1.3.0` advanced to `51bf3db79fdadca26dbde6bbf752c9982c7793e3` with inference-submission budgeting and protocol-schema updates. The context branch was rebased onto that tip; source-tested head is `8d4bb77a1bdb076012e510244f054bc9d4f943f4`. On that head, `make build` passed. Sequential isolated tests passed: `context_inventory.test.ts` 17/17, `knowledge_memory_controls.test.ts` 3/3, `provider_wire_matrix.test.ts` 90/90, `audit_export.test.ts` 18/18, and `session_durable_fields.test.ts` 4/4. The initial inventory attempt failed because this worktree's custom in-repository Cargo target contained an oversized generated `libgit2.a`, which the candidate fingerprint correctly rejected; after removing that task-generated directory and rerunning with the normal ignored build outputs, inventory passed 17/17. The evidence-only documentation commit followed that tested source head. The pre-rebase full-suite failure remains the latest full-run evidence and is not represented as qualification of `8d4bb77a`.\n\nThe exact-head full run on predecessor `361a744f` completed `make test` with exit 2: core Rust 53/53, TUI 406/406, scope fixtures passed, and security lifecycle gates passed. CLI shard one completed 2,970 tests (2,949 passed, 8 failed, 13 skipped) before the 300-second per-file runner killed `compaction_continuity.test.ts`; shard two completed 2,699 tests (2,693 passed, 2 failed, 4 skipped) before the same runner killed `verify_hostile.test.ts`. Running `compaction_continuity.test.ts` directly on that exact snapshot passed 34/34 in 532.959 seconds, confirming that its declared workload exceeds the runner's 300-second default. The two T3 evaluator failures and several evaluator harness failures refused qualification because compiler exposure could not be checked; another evaluator test failed to find the selected stimulus, and a TUI cancellation timing assertion failed. Those failures remain recorded and unclassified; the second timeout has not been independently reproduced. The full suite must be rerun on current head, and the test-runner timeout mismatch and evaluator failures remain qualification issues. The four maturity ratings do not change. No provider was modified; no push, PR, merge, or tag was created.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-24, corrected full suite at release snapshot\n\nThe exact feature snapshot `2b623b4473271111614a80a551b47bfce4297c47` is based on the requested `release/v1.3.0` line. Before this validation, the first full-suite attempt failed evaluator fixtures because the physical checkout had materialized `eval/scan-compiler-exposure.mjs` as group-writable, which the evaluator correctly rejects; two long CLI files also exceeded the default 300-second per-file timeout. The Git-recorded mode was `100644`. For the corrected run, only the snapshot's local materialized mode was restored to `0644` and the timeout was raised to 900 seconds; no tracked source was changed. The evaluator and long-file cases were rerun, then the complete `make test` was executed serially under the SUITE claim with isolated HOME, worktree-local Cargo output, and unique temporary fixture roots.\n\nResults from the corrected run: Rust core tests passed 53/53, Rust TUI tests passed 406/406, and the remaining Rust integration groups passed 9/9. The CLI suite reported 3,053 tests: 3,052 passed, 0 failed, and 1 skipped. Terminal acceptance reported 3,111 tests: 3,095 passed, 0 failed, and 16 skipped. The terminal scope fixtures passed. Every lifecycle security gate reported success, including Node and Rust dependency audits, credential scanning, diff hygiene, and forbidden-documentation scanning. The run output contained no Makefile `[!] FAILED` step marker or final `make test: one or more steps failed` message.\n\nThe outer validation command nevertheless returned exit code 1 after the security gates printed success, with `zsh:42: PIPESTATUS[0]: parameter not set`. Thus the component summaries are green, but the aggregate shell-wrapper status was not captured cleanly; this record does not label the aggregate command as a verified exit-0 run. The exact command output is retained at `/tmp/context-maturity-make-test-2b623b44-fixed.log`. The command's own evaluator tmpfs and runtime roots were removed after its process exited; the separate isolated HOME is retained for diagnostics. The owner checkout and the shared fixture paths were not modified.\n\nThese results supersede the older predecessor-only suite reports as the most recent full-suite evidence, but establish local component qualification only. The maturity ratings remain Navigability 2 — Exploratory, Retrieval speed 2 — Exploratory, Freshness 1 — Ad hoc, and Compounding value 1 in the no-memory fixture / 2 only when durable context is observed. There is still no held-out evidence that durable memory improves task outcomes, no measured retrieval SLO or end-to-end latency distribution, and durable-memory freshness remains unknown where source fingerprints are unavailable. Hosted checks, installed acceptance, independent review, and a fresh full run whose outer command exits zero remain outstanding. No inference provider was modified, and no push, PR, merge, or tag was performed.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-24, current rebased release snapshot\n\nOn implementation head `52b54573264a1ac563119a9f318e57affc3a2753`, based on `release/v1.3.0` at `d03e6199f37bd024f17a807f00ed261291f8a02c`, `make build` passed in an isolated snapshot. The subsequent exact-head `make test` completed its Rust and terminal-security stages but failed in the second CLI shard. Rust core passed 53/53, Rust TUI passed 406/406, and the other Rust integration groups passed (1/1, 3/3, 4/4, and 1/1); the first CLI shard reported 3,108/3,112 passed with 4 skipped. The second CLI shard reported 3,057 tests: 3,043 passed, 1 failed, and 13 skipped. Its sole failure was `test/turn_semantics.test.ts`, “the same trajectory with a passing verifier is VERIFIED only through the verifier run”: expected `execution === COMPLETED`, received `FAILED`. Terminal scope fixtures passed and all lifecycle security gates printed success, but `make test` ended with `make: *** [Makefile:67: test] Error 1`; this is a failed aggregate run.\n\nThe complete `turn_semantics.test.ts` file was then run alone on the same snapshot with an isolated HOME and passed 37/37 in 94.2 seconds. This non-reproduction does not erase the full-suite failure or establish its cause. The exact full-run log is `/tmp/context-maturity-make-test-52b54573-built.log`. No source was changed during these runs. The release branch has since advanced beyond this tested base; the implementation must be rebased and requalified at its current tip before making a release-level claim. Hosted checks, installed acceptance, independent review, and a clean exact-current full-suite run remain outstanding. No live inference was used and no inference provider, PR, release tag, or remote branch was changed.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-24, corrected full suite on snapshot `7b04317a`\n\nThe exact tested commit was `7b04317ab34bacdbd4ccd29511aff25a7aa647f8`, whose release base was `2dea217937aa7b49a0719b4bbfe3d9a9f9658acd`. A detached snapshot was built successfully with `make build`, an isolated HOME, and worktree-local build outputs. The first suite attempt on this snapshot had evaluator preflight failures because the checkout materialized `eval/scan-compiler-exposure.mjs` as group-writable even though Git records mode `100644`; the corrected snapshot restored the physical mode to `0644` without changing tracked files. The corrected `make test` used `NEXUS_TEST_FILE_TIMEOUT_SECS=900` for the existing long integration files and exited 0.\n\nResults: Rust core 53/53, Rust TUI 406/406, other Rust integration groups 9/9; CLI shards 2,724 tests (2,722 passed, 0 failed, 2 skipped) and 3,452 tests (3,437 passed, 0 failed, 15 skipped), totaling 6,176 CLI tests (6,159 passed, 0 failed, 17 skipped). Terminal scope measurement fixtures passed. All lifecycle security gates passed, including Node/Rust dependency checks, credential scanning, diff hygiene, and documentation scanning. The two CLI shard fixture failures visible in the log are the intentional failing-child cases exercised by `test_shards.test.ts`; the shard test suite itself passed. The exact log is `/tmp/context-maturity-make-test-7b04317a-fixed.log`; the command reported `MAKE_TEST_EXIT=0`. The isolated HOME is retained for diagnostics, and the run's unique evaluator mount/runtime paths were removed.\n\nThis result supersedes the earlier failed run on `52b54573` as the newest complete local full-suite result, but it qualifies only commit `7b04317a` and its `2dea2179` release base. `origin/release/v1.3.0` has since advanced to `5abbc4cbb24a2601b8b1c70acc908a90f73926df`; the implementation still needs rebasing and exact-current requalification. These are local tests, not hosted checks or installed acceptance. The maturity ratings do not change: Navigability 2 — Exploratory, Retrieval speed 2 — Exploratory, Freshness 1 — Ad hoc, and Compounding value 1 in the no-memory fixture / 2 only when durable context is observed. Held-out evidence of memory value, a measured retrieval SLO, independent review, and installed acceptance remain outstanding. No inference-provider modification or live inference was used; no push, PR, merge, or tag was performed.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":641609488}},"started_at_ms":1795503030744,"completed_at_ms":1795503031386}}
{"timestamp":"2026-11-24T06:50:31.532Z","ordinal":108350,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a20c52-04ec-7962-9864-8f9317e07697","call_id":"call_cbc8ba30f19944958fafa385e614de20","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 14185)\nTotal output lines: 211\n\n# Nexus context maturity and inventory\n\nStatus: implemented diagnostic, provenance, and request-observability layer on `release/v1.3.0`. Exact content-free request manifests are captured at the existing adapter-dispatch boundary; they do not replace request construction, token admission, or provider adapters.\n\n## Baseline\n\nRepository: Nexus Code. The initial operator checkout was clean `main` at `9156571f7d5fd8f8436c3105f0237628ab35e857`; the requested implementation target is `release/v1.3.0`, initially at `79ebb92c7254340effd5cd296181794d665648ae`. The isolated branch `codex/context-maturity-v130` was rebased as `release/v1.3.0` advanced, most recently onto `7ed3905930551be82b91f11a2aa4abfeec96a99f` (PR #748). That rebase completed without conflicts. The nested-Make classification fix, regression test, skill-scope freshness correction, and qualification updates are committed. Four untracked local trace files remain under `cli/.local/`; they are preserved and excluded from commits. The owner checkout remains untouched. Changes remain isolated and unpublished.\n\nThe reference is *The Context Engineering Maturity Model: A Framework for Building Production-Ready Agentic Systems* (Redis, 2025/2026 ebook), especially its four pillars and five descriptive stages (pp. 7–16). Nexus uses four dimensions requested for this assessment and adapts the stage language to a local coding client. Vendor architecture recommendations are not treated as implementation requirements.\n\n| Dimension | Baseline level | Evidence and observed behavior | Gaps and limitations | Next improvement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Navigability | 2 — Exploratory | `AutonomousAgent.requestMessages` carries conversation and pins the durable objective (`cli/src/agent.ts:2318-2338`); `getContextBudget` accounts for messages and tool schemas (`agent.ts:2475-2478`); repository search, skills, and `KnowledgeGraph` exist (`cli/src/knowledge.ts:37-45`, `cli/src/skills.ts:498-518`). | These sources do not have a shared inspectable inventory; the prompt combines several sources and history is replayed as messages. Retrieval reasons and cross-source relationships are not reported together. | Add a common inventory format and a read-only inspection command, while explicitly listing uncovered sources. |\n| Retrieval speed | 2 — Exploratory | Deterministic client token estimates, provider-advertised counts where available, admission and bounded compaction exist (`cli/src/tokens.ts:97-158`, `cli/src/agent.ts:3410-3507`). | No source-level retrieval latency budget or SLO is recorded. Admission logs do not measure time spent assembling each source; estimates are not provider measurements. | Record per-source and total inventory-assembly duration; keep it distinct from inference latency and token estimates. |\n| Freshness | 1 — Ad hoc | Context is gathered from the live session; candidate/verification evidence has identity-based invalidation elsewhere in the execution model. | Ordinary transcript/tool observations do not consistently carry a repository fingerprint or invalidation rule. Persisted knowledge has timestamps but no enforced applicability fingerprint. Unknown freshness can look current if rendered as prose. | Attach explicit freshness state and source references; report `unknown` where no applicable fingerprint exists. |\n| Compounding value | 2 — Exploratory | Sessions, compaction archives and durable task state persist across process restarts (`cli/src/session.ts:102-184`); project knowledge persists in `.nexus/knowledge.json` (`cli/src/knowledge.ts:37-45`). | Session history, task summaries, and repository knowledge have different scopes but no unified provenance/retention view. Knowledge auto-extraction is opt-in and trust-gated, but a stored entry is not evidence of correctness. | Preserve source-only inventory metadata in session records and exports; do not elevate model or tool claims to verified facts. |\n\n## Implemented assessment\n\nThe current `/context maturity` output is computed from one content-free on-demand inventory and can change with observed session evidence. On the deterministic command fixture used here, it reports navigability 2 — Exploratory, retrieval speed 2 — Exploratory, freshness 1 — Ad hoc, and compounding value 1 — Ad hoc (no durable memory was present in that fixture). Ratings remain independent and do not claim a managed maturity level.\n\n| Dimension | Current level | Evidence now available | Remaining gap / next improvement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Navigability | 2 — Exploratory | `/context inspect [--json]` reports source identity, scope, trust, freshness, retrieval reason/method, and coverage gaps. Bounded inference manifests reference the exact message positions and tool schemas handed to the adapter; current in-memory system-prompt sections link to `request:message:0`; session records and exports preserve the request references. | Message references identify position and role, not a stable semantic relationship graph. Prompt-section links are invalidated after prompt bytes change and are unavailable after resume until the prompt is rebuilt. The manifest proves the client-side handoff attempt, not provider receipt or processing. |\n| Retrieval speed | 2 — Exploratory | Inspection reports per-source and total local collection timings, a configurable total deadline (default 2 seconds), timeout/cancellation outcomes, item omissions, and client-estimated tokens. Request manifests report configured/advertised limits; the last request count/source remains separate when available. Manifest construction now computes the aggregate request estimate while collecting per-message details, avoiding a second full tokenization pass. A synthetic alternating microbenchmark (64 messages, 12 tools, 4 iterations per sample, 8 samples) measured median 54.37 ms for the previous two-pass path and 26.99 ms for the current path (50.4% lower local manifest-build time). | The microbenchmark does not establish end-to-end TUI throughput or inference latency. Diagnostic collection still does not measure live request preparation distributions; synchronous readers cannot be preempted; no cache telemetry or measured retrieval SLO exists. |\n| Freshness | 1 — Ad hoc | Items carry explicit `current` / `stale` / `unknown` plus invalidation rules. Session and archive reconstruction marks history unknown. `/context inspect` records a bounded composite candidate fingerprint and compares it with the previous saved inventory; changed workspaces produce invalidated repository-item references and an explicit partial-coverage reason. Durable knowledge entries now explicitly report unknown freshness in inspection and prompt text because their opaque source references have no stored fingerprint or revalidation. | There is no per-source freshness identity for ignored environment inputs, tool policy, or individual memory provenance, and no measured change-to-invalidation objective. Memory freshness remains unknown; the system does not infer staleness or correctness from an opaque reference. |\n| Compounding value | 1 — Ad hoc in the no-memory fixture and when only a project-knowledge catalog entry is present; 2 — Exploratory only when a durable note or compaction link is observed in carried session context | Session state, compaction links, and bounded request manifests persist source references without copying context text. Operator-only promotion preserves provenance, unverified validation state, repository scope and retention; legacy entries migrate as unverified; expiry is enforced; model writes and automatic extraction are disabled. | Held-out evidence that persistent memory improves engineering outcomes is not measured; the existing durable knowledge store has repository scope only. |\n\nRatings are per dimension. There is no aggregate maturity grade. A feature's presence alone does not satisfy a stage: the report requires evidence for shared behavior, freshness, and measured service levels.\n\n## Design decision record\n\n### Decision\n\nAdd a small, local context inventory and deterministic maturity assessment that reuse `AutonomousAgent` getters, `DurableTaskState`/`SessionManager`, existing token estimates, `KnowledgeGraph`, and the common slash router. The inventory stores metadata and token estimates, not prompt plaintext or content hashes. Existing provider adapters remain the only inference transport boundary; this feature does not call providers.\n\n### Data model and interfaces\n\n- `ContextInventoryItem`: stable source-identity id (source type + location + scope; no content hash is persisted), source kind/location, estimated tokens, observed time, freshness (`current`, `stale`, `unknown`), trust state, retention, inclusion reason, retrieval method, and optional source references. Message and tool-call identifiers are retained only as opaque hashes.\n- `ContextInventorySnapshot`: schema version, capture time, session/task/workspace identity, optional bounded composite workspace fingerprint and comparison outcome, item list, per-source timing/status/counts, estimate provenance, omissions, completeness, and reason for incomplete or unknown coverage. Timeout/cancellation is an additive `outcome` value while the existing `status` remains `failed`, so schema-1 readers continue to accept the inventory.\n- `InferenceContextManifest`: bounded latest-24 session records created immediately before `submitGuardingLength` calls the adapter. It stores message position/role references, effective tool-schema positions/counts, client token estimate, context/output budgets and their sources, and dispatch outcome. Provider token-count observations remain in the existing separate token-accounting record because the code does not bind every such measurement to the exact same serialized arrays. Prompt and schema text, raw request IDs, model/provider identity, and content hashes are not persisted.\n- The persisted snapshot is optional. Older session records remain valid; inventory reconstruction labels transcript freshness unknown and marks session-save coverage partial because the exact provider request is unavailable.\n- `/context inspect [--json] [--record] [--budget-ms <1..30000>] [--max-items <1..500>]` measures current client-visible sources on demand and optionally stores a content-free session snapshot; `/context maturity [--json]` accepts the same budget/item limits and emits a deterministic four-dimension assessment with evidence, gaps, limitations, and next improvements. The default total source-collection budget is 2,000 ms. The same budget applies sequentially across the whole collection pass, so source order is deterministic and remaining sources are marked timed out after exhaustion.\n- `AutonomousAgent.getSystemPromptContextSources()` exposes temporary prompt slices derived from the active system message using captured byte offsets. It includes identifiable sections (few-shots, TDD policy, selected role, project rules, skill catalog, and durable project knowledge) plus residual runtime-policy sections, all linked to `request:message:0`. Only source metadata is persisted. A SHA-256 digest is used in memory only to refuse stale offsets; prompt mutation or restore invalidates the slice mapping, with the opaque system message retained as a conservative fallback.\n\n### Compatibility, security, privacy, and retention\n\n- Existing message and session schemas remain readable. New metadata is additive and source-only; full message and tool content remains in the existing session/archive path.\n- Hashes and locators are diagnostic provenance, not proof that a tool observation is true. Model output is labeled model-generated; tool output is labeled unverified unless an existing verifier receipt binds it.\n- No secret-bearing source text or per-file content hashes are copied into the inventory or maturity report. A composite candidate fingerprint supports equality comparison within the saved session; it does not reveal a file path or claim cor…8185 tokens truncated…reaches diagnostics. `context_inventory.test.ts` passed 17/17 and `npm run build` passed with isolated `HOME` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` on this exact head. The full `make test` result above predates this follow-up; it has not been rerun on `40c2194a`. The four maturity ratings do not change. No provider behavior changed.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-23, latest release rebase and full-suite result\n\nThe feature branch was rebased onto `origin/release/v1.3.0` at `56e644cf5f0b6d3c85b82dc3bfec958535ae2493`. On the then-current pre-rebase-follow-up snapshot `cabe335e83b2b4b870856e6edd949ae7d52f1489`, `make build` passed and `make test` completed with exit 2. Core Rust tests passed 53/53; TUI tests passed 406/406; one CLI shard reported 2,959 tests (2,945 passed, 1 failed, 13 skipped), and the other reported 3,179 tests (3,175 passed, 4 skipped). The failing assertion was `test/cloud_providers.test.ts`: operator cancellation during a streaming response exceeded its 2-second bound and failed after the test runner's 300-second timeout. Scope fixtures and lifecycle/security gates passed. This is a recorded failure, not a green full-suite result.\n\nThe cancellation test was then run five times sequentially with isolated `HOME` and ephemeral mock-provider ports; all five passed in 137–147 ms. Source inspection confirms the caller signal is wired into `StreamWatchdog`, which aborts the fetch signal, and `readSse` cancels its reader on error. These repeats do not explain the full-shard timeout, so the intermittent failure remains unresolved and must not be classified as environmental or fixed. On the subsequently rebased current source, focused tests passed: `context_inventory.test.ts` 17/17, `knowledge_memory_controls.test.ts` 3/3, `provider_wire_matrix.test.ts` (all cases passed), session persistence/export/fork test files (all passed), and `audit_export.test.ts` 18/18. `make build` also passed with isolated `HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, and a worktree-local `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`.\n\nThis rebase includes release-branch commits not present in the full-suite snapshot; full-suite status at the exact current head is therefore unverified. The four maturity ratings and their evidence remain unchanged. No inference provider was modified. Hosted checks, installed acceptance, a repeated comparative performance evaluation, and independent review remain outstanding; Claude owns PR review and merge. No push, PR, merge, or tag was performed.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-24, release tip refresh and focused requalification\n\nAfter the full-suite run, `origin/release/v1.3.0` advanced to `51bf3db79fdadca26dbde6bbf752c9982c7793e3` with inference-submission budgeting and protocol-schema updates. The context branch was rebased onto that tip; source-tested head is `8d4bb77a1bdb076012e510244f054bc9d4f943f4`. On that head, `make build` passed. Sequential isolated tests passed: `context_inventory.test.ts` 17/17, `knowledge_memory_controls.test.ts` 3/3, `provider_wire_matrix.test.ts` 90/90, `audit_export.test.ts` 18/18, and `session_durable_fields.test.ts` 4/4. The initial inventory attempt failed because this worktree's custom in-repository Cargo target contained an oversized generated `libgit2.a`, which the candidate fingerprint correctly rejected; after removing that task-generated directory and rerunning with the normal ignored build outputs, inventory passed 17/17. The evidence-only documentation commit followed that tested source head. The pre-rebase full-suite failure remains the latest full-run evidence and is not represented as qualification of `8d4bb77a`.\n\nThe exact-head full run on predecessor `361a744f` completed `make test` with exit 2: core Rust 53/53, TUI 406/406, scope fixtures passed, and security lifecycle gates passed. CLI shard one completed 2,970 tests (2,949 passed, 8 failed, 13 skipped) before the 300-second per-file runner killed `compaction_continuity.test.ts`; shard two completed 2,699 tests (2,693 passed, 2 failed, 4 skipped) before the same runner killed `verify_hostile.test.ts`. Running `compaction_continuity.test.ts` directly on that exact snapshot passed 34/34 in 532.959 seconds, confirming that its declared workload exceeds the runner's 300-second default. The two T3 evaluator failures and several evaluator harness failures refused qualification because compiler exposure could not be checked; another evaluator test failed to find the selected stimulus, and a TUI cancellation timing assertion failed. Those failures remain recorded and unclassified; the second timeout has not been independently reproduced. The full suite must be rerun on current head, and the test-runner timeout mismatch and evaluator failures remain qualification issues. The four maturity ratings do not change. No provider was modified; no push, PR, merge, or tag was created.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-24, corrected full suite at release snapshot\n\nThe exact feature snapshot `2b623b4473271111614a80a551b47bfce4297c47` is based on the requested `release/v1.3.0` line. Before this validation, the first full-suite attempt failed evaluator fixtures because the physical checkout had materialized `eval/scan-compiler-exposure.mjs` as group-writable, which the evaluator correctly rejects; two long CLI files also exceeded the default 300-second per-file timeout. The Git-recorded mode was `100644`. For the corrected run, only the snapshot's local materialized mode was restored to `0644` and the timeout was raised to 900 seconds; no tracked source was changed. The evaluator and long-file cases were rerun, then the complete `make test` was executed serially under the SUITE claim with isolated HOME, worktree-local Cargo output, and unique temporary fixture roots.\n\nResults from the corrected run: Rust core tests passed 53/53, Rust TUI tests passed 406/406, and the remaining Rust integration groups passed 9/9. The CLI suite reported 3,053 tests: 3,052 passed, 0 failed, and 1 skipped. Terminal acceptance reported 3,111 tests: 3,095 passed, 0 failed, and 16 skipped. The terminal scope fixtures passed. Every lifecycle security gate reported success, including Node and Rust dependency audits, credential scanning, diff hygiene, and forbidden-documentation scanning. The run output contained no Makefile `[!] FAILED` step marker or final `make test: one or more steps failed` message.\n\nThe outer validation command nevertheless returned exit code 1 after the security gates printed success, with `zsh:42: PIPESTATUS[0]: parameter not set`. Thus the component summaries are green, but the aggregate shell-wrapper status was not captured cleanly; this record does not label the aggregate command as a verified exit-0 run. The exact command output is retained at `/tmp/context-maturity-make-test-2b623b44-fixed.log`. The command's own evaluator tmpfs and runtime roots were removed after its process exited; the separate isolated HOME is retained for diagnostics. The owner checkout and the shared fixture paths were not modified.\n\nThese results supersede the older predecessor-only suite reports as the most recent full-suite evidence, but establish local component qualification only. The maturity ratings remain Navigability 2 — Exploratory, Retrieval speed 2 — Exploratory, Freshness 1 — Ad hoc, and Compounding value 1 in the no-memory fixture / 2 only when durable context is observed. There is still no held-out evidence that durable memory improves task outcomes, no measured retrieval SLO or end-to-end latency distribution, and durable-memory freshness remains unknown where source fingerprints are unavailable. Hosted checks, installed acceptance, independent review, and a fresh full run whose outer command exits zero remain outstanding. No inference provider was modified, and no push, PR, merge, or tag was performed.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-24, current rebased release snapshot\n\nOn implementation head `52b54573264a1ac563119a9f318e57affc3a2753`, based on `release/v1.3.0` at `d03e6199f37bd024f17a807f00ed261291f8a02c`, `make build` passed in an isolated snapshot. The subsequent exact-head `make test` completed its Rust and terminal-security stages but failed in the second CLI shard. Rust core passed 53/53, Rust TUI passed 406/406, and the other Rust integration groups passed (1/1, 3/3, 4/4, and 1/1); the first CLI shard reported 3,108/3,112 passed with 4 skipped. The second CLI shard reported 3,057 tests: 3,043 passed, 1 failed, and 13 skipped. Its sole failure was `test/turn_semantics.test.ts`, “the same trajectory with a passing verifier is VERIFIED only through the verifier run”: expected `execution === COMPLETED`, received `FAILED`. Terminal scope fixtures passed and all lifecycle security gates printed success, but `make test` ended with `make: *** [Makefile:67: test] Error 1`; this is a failed aggregate run.\n\nThe complete `turn_semantics.test.ts` file was then run alone on the same snapshot with an isolated HOME and passed 37/37 in 94.2 seconds. This non-reproduction does not erase the full-suite failure or establish its cause. The exact full-run log is `/tmp/context-maturity-make-test-52b54573-built.log`. No source was changed during these runs. The release branch has since advanced beyond this tested base; the implementation must be rebased and requalified at its current tip before making a release-level claim. Hosted checks, installed acceptance, independent review, and a clean exact-current full-suite run remain outstanding. No live inference was used and no inference provider, PR, release tag, or remote branch was changed.\n\n### Validation update — 2026-11-24, corrected full suite on snapshot `7b04317a`\n\nThe exact tested commit was `7b04317ab34bacdbd4ccd29511aff25a7aa647f8`, whose release base was `2dea217937aa7b49a0719b4bbfe3d9a9f9658acd`. A detached snapshot was built successfully with `make build`, an isolated HOME, and worktree-local build outputs. The first suite attempt on this snapshot had evaluator preflight failures because the checkout materialized `eval/scan-compiler-exposure.mjs` as group-writable even though Git records mode `100644`; the corrected snapshot restored the physical mode to `0644` without changing tracked files. The corrected `make test` used `NEXUS_TEST_FILE_TIMEOUT_SECS=900` for the existing long integration files and exited 0.\n\nResults: Rust core 53/53, Rust TUI 406/406, other Rust integration groups 9/9; CLI shards 2,724 tests (2,722 passed, 0 failed, 2 skipped) and 3,452 tests (3,437 passed, 0 failed, 15 skipped), totaling 6,176 CLI tests (6,159 passed, 0 failed, 17 skipped). Terminal scope measurement fixtures passed. All lifecycle security gates passed, including Node/Rust dependency checks, credential scanning, diff hygiene, and documentation scanning. The two CLI shard fixture failures visible in the log are the intentional failing-child cases exercised by `test_shards.test.ts`; the shard test suite itself passed. The exact log is `/tmp/context-maturity-make-test-7b04317a-fixed.log`; the command reported `MAKE_TEST_EXIT=0`. The isolated HOME is retained for diagnostics, and the run's unique evaluator mount/runtime paths were removed.\n\nThis result supersedes the earlier failed run on `52b54573` as the newest complete local full-suite result, but it qualifies only commit `7b04317a` and its `2dea2179` release base. `origin/release/v1.3.0` has since advanced to `5abbc4cbb24a2601b8b1c70acc908a90f73926df`; the implementation still needs rebasing and exact-current requalification. These are local tests, not hosted checks or installed acceptance. The maturity ratings do not change: Navigability 2 — Exploratory, Retrieval speed 2 — Exploratory, Freshness 1 — Ad hoc, and Compounding value 1 in the no-memory fixture / 2 only when durable context is observed. Held-out evidence of memory value, a measured retrieval SLO, independent review, and installed acceptance remain outstanding. No inference-provider modification or live inference was used; no push, PR, merge, or tag was performed.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a20c49-1bcf-7563-9889-443e19f9956a","create_time":1795503031.5327766}},"metadata":{"history_sequence":48711,"sampled_through_sequence":48708,"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
