{"timestamp":"2026-11-24T07:45:42.392Z","ordinal":109563,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1f54f-2cb2-7201-ad27-acf2d9bbd90e","turn_id":"01a20c6e-33b2-7660-a7f0-668b82778aee","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-fa570621-9513-445a-bcf1-ed1cd30cd4c7","process_id":"32969","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '112,135p' docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md; common=$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir); for f in \"$common\"/nexus-claims/FILE_docs_v1.3.0_PLAN.md/owner.json \"$common\"/nexus-claims/FILE_docs_v1.3.0_PLAN.md.released-20261123T-context-maturity-complete/owner.json; do if test -f \"$f\"; then echo \"$f\"; cat \"$f\"; fi; done"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/context-maturity-v130","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"sed -n '112,135p' docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md; common=$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir); for f in \"$common\"/nexus-claims/FILE_docs_v1.3.0_PLAN.md/owner.json \"$common\"/nexus-claims/FILE_docs_v1.3.0_PLAN.md.released-20261123T-context-maturity-complete/owner.json; do if test -f \"$f\"; then echo \"$f\"; cat \"$f\"; fi; done"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"| PA-4 | **Bounded delegation and workflow commands** (trail 428; [baseline](PA4_BASELINE.md)): extend transient `spawn_subagent` and durable operations with a shared validated delegation contract/outcome, enforce authority intersection and recursive/resource limits, then expose real `/goal`, `/loop`, and `/batch` lifecycles through REPL and TUI/sidecar. Reuse `TeamOrchestrator`, `ConvergenceScheduler`, `OperationStore`, `ResourceRegistry`, specialist profiles, provider recovery, completion evidence, and existing stagnation detectors; do not add a second scheduler or workflow store. `/goal` owns an immutable objective and requirement/evidence ledger; `/loop` is bounded and verifier-driven; `/batch` validates a task graph and maps it into existing work units. Parsing, cancellation, pause/resume, status, recovery, and provider-neutral fixtures are part of each command contract. Spec-format validation may proceed independently; lifecycle and shared-contract work depends on PA-1/PA-2 evidence and WP-P10-02, with `/goal` semantics aligned to PA-3. | prompt-only subagent compatibility; parent/profile/session authority intersection and enforced recursive limits; typed non-success and unknown-effect outcomes; each command has durable state, lifecycle controls, recovery and status through supported interfaces; dependency and workspace conflicts cannot silently complete or overwrite; deterministic verifiers and independent review gate acceptance; standard-contract mock plus existing local/cloud fixtures pass without provider changes | L (staged packages) | P1 |\n\n### CE: context engineering maturity (new trail, opened 2026-11-23)\n\n| Unit | What | Accept | Size | Pri |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CE-1 | Establish auditable context capabilities across navigability, retrieval speed, freshness, and compounding value. Reuse request construction, token accounting, session/archive persistence, path-scoped skills, and the existing project knowledge store. Record content-free manifests for the exact client message/tool arrays sent to adapters; keep source provenance, measurements, scope, trust, and freshness separate; make partial/unavailable context visible; govern durable-memory promotion and retention; produce four independent, evidence-backed maturity assessments. See [`docs/research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md`](../research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md). No Redis, external memory service, provider extension, or new scheduler is presumed. | provenance survives request dispatch, compaction, resume, and export; incomplete sources, stale/unknown freshness, budget limits, and persistence failures are explicit; no prompt/tool plaintext or unverified memory is presented as evidence; scoped memory promotion is controlled; local and cloud-compatible mock adapters run without provider changes; each maturity level cites reproducible client evidence and retains open criteria; qualification includes retrieval latency, freshness invalidation, memory-quality, security, and recovery measurements before any managed/competitive-asset claim | M (staged implementation and qualification) | P1 |\n\n## 5. Sequencing\n\nPhase A (foundation, parallelisable): PF-1, PF-2, GV-4, VR-1, GV-3. These unblock the rest and cost little.\nPhase B: AC-1, HK-1, GV-1, GV-2, PF-4, PA-2, VR-2, MT-1, VR-3, SB-1.\nPhase C: BG-1, BG-2, HK-2, PF-3, AC-2, PA-1, GV-5, MT-2.\nPhase D (stretch, may roll to v1.4): PA-3, HK-3, BG-3.\nPA-4 is staged: the existing operation-spec validator can be reused independently; command lifecycles and shared delegation changes follow WP-P10-02 and verified PA-1/PA-2 gates. `/goal` must also satisfy PA-3 before promotion.\nMT-2 follows MT-1 so the registry refactor targets the split spine; SB-1 may land beside GV-1 (profiles must not outrank managed policy). VR-3 can ship as soon as sandbox denials are stable under SB-1 aliases, or earlier against today's engines if the receipt shape is profile-agnostic.\nCE-1 is a new context-quality trail. Its inventory, guarded memory controls, request manifests, bounded collection budget, and repository-fingerprint comparison are implemented on the `release/v1.3.0` work branch. An earlier full `make test` passed on detached snapshot `eda532061b8f78c67ddc3ffe4e3c3621312fd4eb`, but predates later context changes. On snapshot `361a744f` (release base `56e644cf`), the follow-up full suite exited 2: core Rust 53/53, TUI 406/406, scope fixtures, and security gates passed; both CLI shards timed out, with 10 other reported failures. The context continuity file passed directly 34/34 in 532.959 seconds, longer than the test runner's 300-second file limit. Several evaluator failures refused qualification because compiler exposure could not be checked; all failures and the second shard timeout remain unclassified. The release branch later advanced to `51bf3db7`; source-tested head `8d4bb77a` is rebased there. On this source head, `make build`, context inventory 17/17, memory controls 3/3, provider wire matrix 90/90, audit export 18/18, and session durability 4/4 passed. The subsequent commit only updates qualification documentation; the full suite has not run on the latest source head. See [`CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md`](../research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md) for exact evidence and limitations. Remaining qualification includes full suite on the latest source head, resolution of suite timeout and evaluator failures, independent review, hosted and installed acceptance, source-specific non-repository freshness, measured retrieval percentiles/SLO, and held-out durable-memory value. Memory freshness remains explicitly unknown where its references cannot be revalidated. CE-1 reuses the existing client and session architecture and does not depend on changing inference providers.\n\nDependencies on earlier plans: v1.2.1 RD-3 (`stream-json` output) feeds PF-2; RD-4 (GitHub Action) pairs with VR-1; CS-9 (per-command approval rules, v1.2.2) and GV-1 must agree on rule semantics (allow < prompt < forbidden, deciding rule recorded); MT-1 (the `agent.ts` split) makes AC-1 and BG-1 much easier and should land first if possible; PR-* (provider registry) is independent; MQ-* (measurement) should publish at least one result before this release claims anything.\n\n## 6. Release gates\n\n| Gate | Condition |\n|---|---|\n/home/mike/nexus-code/.git/nexus-claims/FILE_docs_v1.3.0_PLAN.md.released-20261123T-context-maturity-complete/owner.json\n{\"agent\":\"codex-gpt-6\",\"branch\":\"codex/context-maturity-v130\",\"worktree\":\"/home/mike/nexus-wt/context-maturity-v130\",\"since\":\"2026-11-23T02:05:59Z\",\"scope\":\"Maintain the v1.3.0 context maturity workstream and implementation evidence.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":653250529}},"started_at_ms":1795506341738,"completed_at_ms":1795506342391}}
