PA-2 gates. `/goal` must also satisfy PA-3 before promotion.\nMT-2 follows MT-1 so the registry refactor targets the split spine; SB-1 may land beside GV-1 (profiles must not outrank managed policy). VR-3 can ship as soon as sandbox denials are stable under SB-1 aliases, or earlier against today's engines if the receipt shape is profile-agnostic.\nCE-1 is a new context-quality trail. Its inventory, guarded memory controls, request manifests, bounded collection budget, and repository-fingerprint comparison are implemented on the `release/v1.3.0` work branch. An earlier full `make test` passed on detached snapshot `eda532061b8f78c67ddc3ffe4e3c3621312fd4eb`, but predates later context changes. On snapshot `361a744f` (release base `56e644cf`), the follow-up full suite exited 2: core Rust 53/53, TUI 406/406, scope fixtures, and security gates passed; both CLI shards timed out, with 10 other reported failures. The context continuity file passed directly 34/34 in 532.959 seconds, longer than the test runner's 300-second file limit. Several evaluator failures refused qualification because compiler exposure could not be checked; all failures and the second shard timeout remain unclassified. The release branch later advanced to `51bf3db7`; source-tested head `8d4bb77a` is rebased there. On this source head, `make build`, context inventory 17/17, memory controls 3/3, provider wire matrix 90/90, audit export 18/18, and session durability 4/4 passed. The subsequent commit only updates qualification documentation; the full suite has not run on the latest source head. See [`CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md`](../research/context-engineering/CONTEXT-MATURITY-SYSTEM.md) for exact evidence and limitations. Remaining qualification includes full suite on the latest source head, resolution of suite timeout and evaluator failures, independent review, hosted and installed acceptance, source-specific non-repository freshness, measured retrieval percentiles/SLO, and held-out durable-memory value. 